{# Single source of truth for the secondary navigation links. Rendered in the
   desktop page header — where they are split across the header bar and the menu
   behind its ⋯ control — and in the mobile menu tray, which renders both groups in
   sequence. So the same links appear in every placement without divergent markup.

   The glyphs come from the shared icon macro; nav items ask for 20px, against
   the 22px the category tab bar uses. That import lives inside `item()` rather
   than at the top of this file: a Twig macro sees only its own arguments, so a
   file-level import is invisible from inside one. #}

{# One nav item, in the single presentation used by both the header and the tray:
   an icon beside a label. Two labels are rendered, not one — the header is a
   single row sharing its width with a brand of unbounded length (SITE_TITLE is
   user-configurable), so it shows the short form and drops to the icon alone in
   a narrow band; the tray always shows the full form. CSS picks; the markup
   carries both. This is the same arrangement the category tabs use.

   Because the visible label can be hidden entirely, it cannot be what names the
   link: every item carries an explicit aria-label holding the full name, and the
   visible spans are hidden from assistive technology. So the label shortens and
   the accessible name never does.

   `current` names the destination being viewed. That item renders as a span
   rather than an anchor: a link pointing at the page you are already on is the
   thing being avoided, and removing the href is what actually removes it.

   `action` is the other way an entry can end: not a destination at all, but an
   Alpine expression run in the enclosing scope. Support Development is the one
   entry that takes it — it opens an overlay over the current page. Such an entry
   renders as a real <button>, and that is not cosmetic. An <a> with no href is
   neither focusable nor announced as anything, and an <a href="#"> announces a
   destination and dirties the URL; either would leave a keyboard user with an
   entry the sighted user can see and they cannot reach. The classes are unchanged
   because .btn.nav-item already dresses a non-anchor — logout_link() below is a
   form-submit control wearing exactly these. #}
{% macro item(key, href, label, short, current, external = false, action = '') %}
    {% import 'partials/_icons.html.twig' as icons %}
    {% set body %}
        <span class="nav-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.icon(key, 20) }}</span>
        <span class="nav-label" aria-hidden="true">{{ label }}</span>
        <span class="nav-label nav-label--short" aria-hidden="true">{{ short }}</span>
    {% endset %}
    {# data-tooltip-collapsed keeps the bubble silent while a label is on screen,
       so it only names the action in the narrow band where the header is down to
       icons alone. #}
    {% if action %}
        {# No `current` branch: an entry that opens an overlay has no page to be
           on, so there is nothing for `current` to match. #}
        <button type="button" class="btn nav-item" @click="{{ action }}" aria-label="{{ label }}"
                data-tooltip="{{ label }}" data-tooltip-collapsed>{{ body }}</button>
    {% elseif key == current %}
        <span class="btn nav-item nav-item--current" aria-current="page" aria-label="{{ label }}"
              data-tooltip="{{ label }}" data-tooltip-collapsed>{{ body }}</span>
    {% else %}
        <a class="btn nav-item" href="{{ href }}" aria-label="{{ label }}"
           data-tooltip="{{ label }}" data-tooltip-collapsed
           {%- if external %} target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}
           {%- if key == 'import' %} data-import{% elseif key == 'orphans' %} data-orphans
           {%- elseif key == 'settings' %} data-settings{% endif %}>{{ body }}</a>
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# The secondary links, in two groups.

   The desktop header renders `bar_links()` in the header bar and `overflow_links()`
   inside the menu behind the ⋯ control; the phone tray renders both in sequence, so
   all six entries are still one flat list there and the order is unchanged.
   Splitting the macro rather than the markup is what keeps the single source of
   truth: neither placement gets to name a link the other does not.

   The split is a width problem answered with a hierarchy. The header's contents are
   aligned to the 960px content column rather than the viewport, and share it with a
   brand of unbounded length (SITE_TITLE is user-configurable) — six labelled items
   plus the connection status runs 824–909px of that, so the bar was one long title
   from crowding. The line drawn is what acts on your poster library against what
   does not: Wall, Import and Orphans are why you opened Marquee; Settings, Support
   and Log out are configuration, an external ask, and the way out. #}
{% macro bar_links(current = '') %}
    {# Opens in a new tab, unlike the two beside it, and still belongs here: it is
       the feature people open the app to show someone. Demoting it a click to save
       ~80px is not a trade worth making. #}
    {{ _self.item('wall', '/wall', 'Poster Wall', 'Wall', current, true) }}
    {{ _self.item('import', '/plex', 'Import from Plex', 'Import', current) }}
    {{ _self.item('orphans', '/orphans', 'Orphans', 'Orphans', current) }}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro overflow_links(current = '', signed_in = false) %}
    {{ _self.item('settings', '/settings', 'Settings', 'Settings', current) }}
    {# Not part of the poster workflow, so it sits last among the destinations —
       and it is no longer a destination at all. It used to open the project's
       support page in a new tab, like Poster Wall; it now opens the ask itself
       over the current page. `supportOpen` is declared on the topnav scope in
       layout.html.twig, which encloses both placements of this group. #}
    {{ _self.item('support', '', 'Support Development', 'Support', current, false, 'supportOpen = true') }}
    {# Last, and gated: it ends the session rather than going anywhere. The gate is
       an argument rather than the caller's `{% verbatim %}{% if %}{% endverbatim %}`
       so that "which links are in this group" is answered in exactly one place —
       `holds()` below has to agree with it. #}
    {% if signed_in %}
        {{ _self.logout_link() }}
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# The "more actions" mark, worn by the phone's menu button and by the desktop
   header's overflow control alike, so one affordance means one thing at both
   widths: the rest of the actions are behind this. On a phone it holds all six
   entries, on desktop the three the bar does not carry.

   Not in _icons.html.twig, and not for want of trying to put it there: every glyph
   in that file is one drawing style — no fill, a currentColor stroke at 1.7 — and
   three filled dots is not that. Rendering it through `icon()` would mean either
   stroking three tiny circles, which reads as rings at this size, or giving that
   macro an exception for one mark. #}
{% macro overflow_glyph(size = 24) %}
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="{{ size }}" height="{{ size }}" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <circle cx="5" cy="12" r="1.9" fill="currentColor"/>
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="1.9" fill="currentColor"/>
        <circle cx="19" cy="12" r="1.9" fill="currentColor"/>
    </svg>
{% endmacro %}

{# The current-page class for the ⋯ control, when the destination being viewed is
   one the overflow menu holds.

   Defined here, beside the group, because it exists to keep that control marked as
   the current page while the entry that says so is hidden behind a click. A list
   maintained anywhere else would drift from the group the first time either moved.
   Log out is absent deliberately: it is an action with no page to be on. So is
   Support Development, for the same reason since it became an overlay — leaving
   it in this list would invite a `nav_current` of 'support' that marked the ⋯
   control as the current page for something that is not a page.

   It emits the class rather than answering yes/no, and that is not a stylistic
   choice. A Twig macro returns a `Markup` object, and an object is always truthy in
   PHP — so `{% verbatim %}{{ nav.x(...) ? ' cls' }}{% endverbatim %}` would add the
   class on every page, marking the control current everywhere. Printing the answer
   sidesteps the cast entirely. #}
{% macro overflow_current_class(current = '') %}
    {{- current == 'settings' ? ' nav-item--current' : '' -}}
{% endmacro %}

{# The Plex connection, as a status rather than a destination.

   It replaced a plain "Plex Connection" nav item sitting among the poster
   actions, which read as somewhere to go rather than something to know. There is
   nothing to do on that screen day to day — you connect once — so what is worth
   carrying in the header is whether Marquee can still reach Plex, not a door.

   It stays a link because it is the only way to reach Disconnect. Removing the
   item entirely would have left that action reachable by typing a URL.

   Naming: the accessible name says "Plex connection" and then the state, because
   a server name alone ("Anansi") tells a screen reader nothing about what the
   control is for. The visible label is the server name, which is the part a
   sighted user is scanning for. #}
{% macro connection_status(connection, current = '') %}
    {% set connected = connection.isConnected %}
    {# "Connected", not "Plex", when the server has not told us what it calls
       itself: naming the product where a server name belongs reads as the name
       of something. It is cached at sign-in, so this is the rare case of a
       deleted database, not the ordinary one. #}
    {% set name = connected ? (connection.serverName ?? 'Connected') : 'Not connected' %}
    {% set label = 'Plex connection: ' ~ (connected ? 'connected to ' ~ name : 'not connected') %}
    {# A lit dot and a name, deliberately not the icon-and-label shape the items
       above use. Carrying no glyph is what separates a reading from an action:
       every control in this bar wears one, so wearing one made this look like a
       sixth place to go. The dot is the whole indicator. #}
    {% set body %}
        <span class="conn-dot conn-dot--{{ connected ? 'ok' : 'off' }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="conn-status__name" aria-hidden="true">{{ name }}</span>
    {% endset %}
    {% if current == 'connect' %}
        <span class="conn-status conn-status--current" aria-current="page"
              aria-label="{{ label }}" data-tooltip="{{ label }}">{{ body }}</span>
    {% else %}
        <a class="conn-status" href="/connect"
           aria-label="{{ label }}" data-tooltip="{{ label }}">{{ body }}</a>
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# Log out. Presented exactly like the links above rather than as a plain text
   link, so the header reads as one set of actions instead of a group plus a
   stray. Callers gate it on signed_in().

   Just the two words. It carried a sentence about the Plex connection surviving,
   which the tray renders as visible text — so the one control a user reaches for
   most became the largest thing in the menu, to explain something they were not
   asking about yet. That belongs where the two exits are weighed against each
   other, which is the connection screen: it offers Disconnect, states what
   Disconnect costs, and says logging out does neither. #}
{% macro logout_link() %}
    {{ _self.item('logout', '/logout', 'Log out', 'Log out', '') }}
{% endmacro %}
